				<h3>Lesson 1: The AI-Driven MEV Double-Edged Sword</h3>
				<p>AI-driven MEV bots have become incredibly sophisticated. They provide a service by reordering transactions for arbitrage, ensuring prices are aligned across protocols. However, they are also predatory, extracting value from users via sandwich attacks and frontrunning. This increases transaction costs and erodes trust.</p>
				<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1677442136019-21780ecad995?auto=format&fit=crop&w=900&q=80" alt="AI trading bot monitoring blockchain transactions on a screen" />

				<h3>Lesson 2: The Oracle is the Single Point of Failure</h3>
				<p>Numerous DeFi hacks originated from manipulated price oracles. Whether through flash-loan attacks on DEX pools or simple data-feed publisher errors, if the data source is flawed, the AI's decisions are flawed, no matter how smart the model is.</p>
				<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516321497487-e288fb19713f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=900&q=80" alt="Secure blockchain infrastructure with connected data nodes" />

				<h3>Lesson 3: Black Box Models Are a Governance Nightmare</h3>
				<p>Some protocols have experimented with AI to set parameters like interest rates or collateralization factors. When a model is a black box, the community cannot understand why it made a specific decision. This makes risk management, debugging, and governance nearly impossible.</p>

				<h3>Lesson 4: Automated Liquidations Can Create Death Spirals</h3>
				<p>AI liquidation bots are essential for healthy lending protocols. However, during market crashes, a cascade of automated liquidations can dump collateral assets on the market, causing prices to crater and triggering even more liquidations.</p>
